# A VARIANT, NEVER THE PRODUCT: THE GHOST OUTLINE BEHIND A THING THAT STANDS STILL ON THE SCREEN OVER A FAST BACKGROUND
# (2026-10-11; docs\reports\fg-3\sitting-2026-10-11b\BRIEF-ghost.md, READING.md 5b to 5e, RESEARCH-disocclusion.md).
# IT CHANGES THE PICTURE. Built only into a variant library (build-ffx-own.ps1 -Variant; tools\ffx-own\build_variants.py).
#
# WHAT AMD'S CODE DOES THERE. Behind the player's car the ground passes fast. The strip the car has just uncovered is in
# ONE real frame; the interpolation pass switches hard between "one frame" and "both" at the render size, and a fetched
# piece of ground arrives with the shade of the place it was fetched from: the strip stands out, a seam in the shape
# of the car's back lies half a ground step behind it, and the frame before's part beyond the seam carries the car's
# own shade out with it.
#
# THE VARIANT, in three passes of AMD's and with no pass added:
#   THE DISOCCLUSION MASK'S PASS (render size) looks along a pixel's motion, in the CURRENT frame's depth, for the first
#     jump towards the camera: an occluder's edge. It looks only for a pixel that moves by three render pixels a half
#     step or more AND that AMD's own mask calls disoccluded, or whose neighbourhood is nearer and moves otherwise (the
#     cheap look: two filtered samples of the game field's own pyramid). The occluder must move otherwise itself (by
#     its own vector, from the current frame's). The distance of each sample's fetch place from that edge, in the
#     sample's own frame, becomes its weight: about nothing within the keep-off, full after one relative half step.
#     The mask grows from two channels to four: the two weights (still exactly 0 or 1 wherever nothing was found),
#     and the occluder's own half vector, which also says "something was found here".
#   THE OPTICAL FLOW FIELD'S PASS (an eighth of the display, 8 by 8 pixels a thread) also writes THE BROAD COLOUR of
#     the current real frame: four filtered taps a block, one in each quarter of it, of the block's FAR layer only (by the
#     frame's depth), and that layer's depth beside it. ONE small image a dispatch; the frame before's is the one the
#     dispatch before wrote (the two images change places at every dispatch). The first interpolated frame after a
#     reset has no such image of the frame before it and takes no broad colour (its weights it takes).
#   THE INTERPOLATION PASS, only where the mask says something was found: (1) each fetched sample takes the broad colour
#     of the place it is put, from its own frame: sample * low(target) / low(fetch), the target displaced by the
#     occluder's half vector, a block left out whose far layer is nearer than the pixel's own surface, the gain held
#     within 1/3 and 3, and only as far as the two real frames agree about the place's broad brightness; (2) the two
#     samples are mixed by their weights, so the switch from one frame to both is a ramp; (3) where the gain would
#     pass its bound the sample fades to the same frame's own pixel at the place (the right shade, the wrong texture
#     phase). Optical flow is kept out as far as the weights are low, as AMD keeps it out of a disoccluded pixel.
# EVERYWHERE ELSE THE PICTURE IS THE PRODUCT'S: the mask's first two channels are AMD's 0 and 1 there, its last two are
# 0, and the interpolation pass takes AMD's own path on that test alone.
#
# NO NEW KIND OF CALL: loads, filtered samples of one level (SampleLevel through AMD's own linear, clamped sampler), plain
# arithmetic; no gather, no wave operation, no atomic.
what A VARIANT (the picture changes): near a nearer thing that moves otherwise, each sample of the interpolation takes the broad colour of the place it is put and is weighed by its distance from that thing in its own frame
passes ffx_frameinterpolation_pass, ffx_frameinterpolation_disocclusion_mask_pass, ffx_frameinterpolation_optical_flow_vector_field_pass
wave64 the same edits in both variants: no wave operation; not run here on a wave64 device
